{{c|''Spherical Trigonometry.''}}

7. We shall throughout assume such elementary propositions in spherical geometry as are required for the purpose of the investigation of formulae given below.

''Definition of Spherical Triangle.'' A spherical triangle is the portion of the surface of a sphere bounded by three arcs of great circles of the sphere. If <math>BC</math>, <math>CA</math>, <math>AB</math> denote these arcs, the circular measure of the angles subtended by these arcs respectively at the centre of the sphere are the sides <math>a</math>, <math>b</math>, <math>c</math> of the spherical triangle <math>ABC</math>; and, if the portions of planes passing through these arcs and the centre of the sphere be drawn, the angles between the portions of planes intersecting at <math>A</math>, <math>B</math>, <math>C</math> respectively are the angles <math>A</math>, <math>B</math>, <math>C</math> of the spherical triangle. It is not necessary to consider triangles in which a side is greater than <math>\pi</math>, since we may replace such a side by the remaining arc of the great circle to which it belongs. ''Associated Triangles.'' Since two great circles intersect each other in two points, there are eight triangles of which the sides are arcs of the same three great circles. If we consider one of these triangles <math>ABC</math> as the fundamental one, then one of the others is equal in all respects to <math>ABC</math>, and the remaining six have each one side equal to, or common with,

