9. ''Formulæ for Sine and Cosine of Half Angles.'' If <math>D</math> be the point of intersection of the three bisectors of the angles <math>A</math>, <math>B</math>, <math>C</math>, and if <math>DE</math> be drawn perpendicular to <math>BC</math>, it may be shown that <math>BE = \tfrac{1}{2}(a + c - b)</math> and <math>CE = \tfrac{1}{2}(a + b - c)</math>, and that the angles <math>BDE</math>, <math>ADC</math> are supplementary. We have also <math>\frac{\sin c}{\sin BD} = \frac{\sin ADB}{\sin \frac{1}{2}A}</math>, <math>\frac{\sin b}{\sin CD} = \frac{\sin ADC}{\sin \frac{1}{2}A}</math>; therefore <math>\sin^2 \tfrac{1}{2}A = \frac{\sin BD \sin CD \sin CDE \sin BDE}{\sin b \sin c}</math>. But <math>\sin BD \sin BDE = \sin BE = \sin \tfrac{1}{2}(a+c-b)</math>, and <math>\sin CD \sin CDE = \sin CE = \sin \tfrac{1}{2}(a+b-c)</math>;
{{block center|therefore <math>\sin\frac{A}{2} = \left\{\frac{\sin \frac{1}{2}(a+c-b) \sin \frac{1}{2}(a+b-c)}{\sin b \sin c}\right\}^{\frac{1}{2}}</math> (5)}}
Apply this formula to the associated triangle of which <math>\pi - A</math>, <math>\pi - B</math>, <math>C</math> are the angles and <math>\pi - a</math>, <math>\pi - b</math>, <math>c</math> are the sides; we obtain
{{block center|the formula <math>\cos\frac{A}{2} = \left\{\frac{\sin \frac{1}{2}(b+c-a) \sin \frac{1}{2}(a+b+c)}{\sin b \sin c}\right\}^{\frac{1}{2}}</math> (6)}}
